<% content_for :title, "Herds — Waterhole" %>

<div class="mb-5">
  <h1 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Herds</h1>
  <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">
    The <%= pluralize(@instances.size, "instance") %> that come down to
    <%= Waterhole::Deployment.host %>.
  </p>
</div>

<ul class="divide-y divide-stone-200 dark:divide-stone-800 overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 bg-white dark:bg-stone-900">
  <% @instances.each do |instance| %>
    <li>
      <%= link_to herd_path(instance),
            class: "flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-3 gap-y-2 px-4 py-3 hover:bg-stone-50 dark:hover:bg-stone-800" do %>
        <span class="font-medium"><%= instance.domain %></span>

        <% if instance == current_instance %>
          <span class="inline-flex rounded-full bg-stone-900 dark:bg-stone-100 px-2 py-0.5 text-xs font-medium text-white dark:text-stone-900">Yours</span>
        <% end %>

        <span class="ml-auto flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
          <%= herd_status_badge(instance) %>
          <%= herd_signals_badge(instance) %>
        </span>
      <% end %>
    </li>
  <% end %>
</ul>

<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-xs text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">
  A herd's status is whatever its own DNS record says, which anyone can look up
  for themselves. Cross-instance signals need two keys — the instance admin's
  <code>signals=on</code> in that record, and this waterhole's operator
  approving it — and they are reciprocal: a herd only sees the signal if it
  contributes to it, so the herds shown as on are exactly the ones yours is
  matched against. Nothing here says anything about anyone's applicants.
</p>
